Good news: I found several concrete bugs. Here's a full breakdown of what's broken and why.

Bug Analysis: Hey Aragorn Colab Notebook
🔴 Critical: Empty Background Noise Directories (Steps 8 & 9)
This is likely the first crash you'll hit. In Step 8, the notebook downloads rirs_noises.zip (for room impulse responses) and creates two directories — fma_16k and audioset_16k — but never puts any audio in them:

python
os.makedirs("audioset_16k", exist_ok=True)
os.makedirs("fma_16k", exist_ok=True)
# ← Nothing is ever downloaded here
Then in Step 9, the Augmentation class references those empty dirs as background_paths with "AddBackgroundNoise": 0.75. When audiomentations tries to load background files at 75% probability and finds zero .wav files, it will throw a hard error. You need to either actually populate those directories (e.g., download FMA/AudioSet audio) or reduce AddBackgroundNoise probability to 0.0 as a workaround until you have real background audio.
​

🔴 Critical: Wrong HuggingFace Dataset Filenames (Step 10)
The notebook tries to download these files:

text
dinner_party.zip, dinner_party_eval.zip, no_speech.zip, speech.zip
But the actual kahrendt/microwakeword HuggingFace dataset uses different names — the dataset page describes files as dinner_party_background, no_speech_background, and speech_background. The exact zip filenames don't match, so urllib.request.urlretrieve will get 404 responses for every file, silently "succeed" (because urlretrieve doesn't raise on HTTP errors by default), and you'll end up with corrupt/empty zip files that unzip will fail on.
​

Fix: Use requests.get() with status code checking, and verify the correct filenames directly from the HuggingFace repo file browser.

🟠 Medium: piper-tts Pip Package Conflicts With Cloned Repo (Step 2)
The notebook installs piper-tts via pip in Step 2, then clones piper-sample-generator separately in Step 3. The pip piper-tts package installs its own piper module namespace and potentially conflicting onnxruntime/numpy dependencies. When Step 6 then runs python -m piper_sample_generator with a custom PYTHONPATH, you may hit namespace collisions or version conflicts between the pip-installed piper-tts and the cloned repo's expectations.
​

Fix: Remove piper-tts from the Step 2 install — it's not needed. The sample generator repo is self-contained.

🟠 Medium: --no-deps on microWakeWord Install (Step 3)
python
subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-m", "pip", "install", "-q", "--no-deps", "-e", "microWakeWord"], check=True)
Using --no-deps means none of microWakeWord's own declared dependencies get installed. Step 2 manually installs several packages, but it likely misses things like librosa, soundfile, or other audio I/O libraries that microWakeWord pulls in. This will surface as ImportError in Step 9 when you import from microwakeword.audio.
​

Fix: Drop --no-deps, or audit microWakeWord/setup.py (or pyproject.toml) and manually add any missing packages to Step 2.

🟡 Minor: Spectrogram Output Path vs. Training Config Mismatch (Steps 9 & 11)
Step 9 saves features to:

text
generated_augmented_features/training/wakeword_mmap
But the training config in Step 11 sets:

python
{"features_dir": "generated_augmented_features", ...}
Depending on how microwakeword.model_train_eval scans for mmap subdirectories, this may or may not resolve correctly. It's worth changing the config features_dir to the full path "generated_augmented_features/training" to be explicit.
​

🟡 Minor: minimization_metric: None in YAML Config (Step 11)
python
"minimization_metric": None,
PyYAML serializes None as null. If the training script expects an empty string "" or a specific sentinel value instead of a YAML null, it may crash with an AttributeError or TypeError when calling string methods on the null.
​

Fix: Change to "minimization_metric": "" or remove the key entirely.

